nodecode-archytan-lite is a rewrite of High-ArchyTech-Solutions's High-ArchyTech-Solutions/hermes-archytan-lite:

  https://github.com/High-ArchyTech-Solutions/hermes-archytan-lite
  commit 38748165b089f776acf9d6119eaeafdb76eed83e
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 High ArchyTech Solutions (LICENSE)

The plugin maps a harness's tool call to the actions it asks for -- the programs
a shell command runs, with its subcommands; the paths it reads, writes and
redirects into; the variables it sets; the hosts it fetches; the tool itself --
and puts every one of them to a gate that answers allow or block, all or nothing,
with per-minute limits, a fail-closed posture and a JSONL decision journal.
This rewrite keeps that: the mapping and the resource-id spelling (git/push,
https/host, a workspace path), the wildcard matching, the all-or-nothing
decision over a call's checks, the per-action window, the unreadable-command
refusal, and the journal. It is Common Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract
instead of a Python plugin: the gate is the policy file the cell reads (and
re-reads when it changes), the hook is the :TOOL point, and the model reaches
the verbs through eval rather than through the harness's plugin API.

Left out: the out-of-process gate itself -- the Docker-provisioned image, the
HTTP authorize/redeem exchange, the Ed25519 keys and the signed receipt chain,
single-use capabilities, and the SIGHUP reload -- because a Nodecode cell
has no second process to trust and no reason to prove a decision to itself; the
policy file, read in process, is the gate. Also left out: the harness's plugin
entry points (pre_tool_call, and the /archytan subcommands that provision
Docker, rotate keys and write a unit) and the interactive allow-once, which
here is the operator's edit of the policy or of the `ungated` list. The journal
is one line per decision and is not rotated at 2 MB.
